\title{Sheaf-Theoretic Data Integration:\\
The Information-Order and Colimit Layer of the Database Sheaf}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-19}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A database with missing entries is naturally modeled as a \emph{partial section of a
sheaf} on the grid of its cells. We develop the order-theoretic skeleton beneath
this view and prove that the informal slogan ``databases form a sheaf'' is, made
precise, an equalizer / colimit statement in the partial order of partial databases
ordered by information (extension). Our central contribution is a sharp
biconditional characterization, \texttt{sheaf\_iff\_common\_extension}: a family of
partial databases admits a single consistent merge \emph{if and only if} it
satisfies the sheaf (pairwise overlap-agreement) condition. We strengthen the
previously known one-directional existence result into this two-way characterization
and add the universal property \texttt{glueFamily\_is\_lub}, which pins the merge
down as a genuine colimit (least common extension). Along the way we establish that
partial databases form a partial order under extension, that the binary gluing is
the join, and that the arbitrary-arity colimit \texttt{glueFamily} extends every
member and is the least such extension. We connect this layer to progressive
imputation and record a quantitative consistency-probability model whose
exponential decay $(1-r)^{C}$ explains why exact global consistency is rare for wide
databases. All results are formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Missing data is ubiquitous: sensors drop readings, respondents skip questions,
instruments are interrupted. The standard response---\emph{imputation}---treats each
missing cell as an isolated estimation problem (mean imputation, $k$-nearest
neighbors, model-based methods such as MICE), implicitly assuming the missing
entries are independent subproblems.

We take a different stance. A database with missing entries is a single geometric
object---a partial section of a \emph{sheaf} on the grid of cells---and the question
``can the holes be filled consistently?'' is exactly the gluing question of sheaf
theory. We show that the database setting realizes this apparatus faithfully and
that the gluing question has a clean, complete answer.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item A partial order on partial databases (the information order $\Extends$),
    proved reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric.
  \item The binary gluing is the join (\texttt{gluing\_is\_lub}), with the free
    ``separatedness'' half \texttt{consistentPair\_of\_common\_extension}.
  \item An arbitrary-arity colimit $\glueFamily$, with its boundary behavior,
    gluing property, and universal property.
  \item The main characterization \texttt{sheaf\_iff\_common\_extension}: a
    consistent merge exists iff the sheaf condition holds.
  \item Connection to progressive imputation: monotone filtrations are automatically
    consistent, and a filtration's colimit is its top level.
  \item A quantitative consistency-probability model with exponential decay
    $(1-r)^{C}$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Fix $\mathit{nRows}, \mathit{nCols} \in \mathbb{N}$ and a value type $V$.

\begin{definition}[Cell grid]
A \emph{position} is a pair $p \in \DBPos := \mathrm{Fin}\,\mathit{nRows} \times
\mathrm{Fin}\,\mathit{nCols}$, indexing one cell of the grid.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partial database]
A \emph{partial database} is a function $db : \DBPos \to \mathrm{Option}\,V$. We
write $db\,p = \some\,v$ when cell $p$ holds value $v$, and $db\,p = \none$ when the
cell is missing. Its \emph{domain} is $\dom(db) = \{p : db\,p \neq \none\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consistent pair]
Databases $db_1, db_2$ form a \emph{consistent pair}, $\ConsistentPair\,db_1\,db_2$,
when
\[
  \forall p,\ \forall v_1\, v_2,\quad db_1\,p = \some\,v_1 \ \wedge\ db_2\,p =
  \some\,v_2 \ \Longrightarrow\ v_1 = v_2.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sheaf condition]
A family $dbs : \iota \to \PartialDB$ satisfies $\SheafCondition\,dbs$ when
$\forall i\,j,\ \ConsistentPair\,(dbs\,i)\,(dbs\,j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gluing map]
$(\Glue\,db_1\,db_2)\,p = \some\,v$ if $db_1\,p = \some\,v$, else $db_2\,p$. It
prefers the first database where both are defined.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Information order]
$\Extends\,\mathit{big}\,\mathit{small}$ holds when $\forall p\,v,\ \mathit{small}\,p
= \some\,v \Rightarrow \mathit{big}\,p = \some\,v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Family colimit]
For $dbs : \iota \to \PartialDB$,
\[
  \glueFamily\,dbs\,p = \begin{cases} dbs\,(\mathrm{choose}\,h)\,p & \text{if } h :
  \exists i,\ dbs\,i\,p \neq \none,\\ \none & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Overlap constraint count and consistency probability]
$\mathrm{overlapConstraintCount}\,n\,\mathit{nRows}\,\mathit{nCols} =
\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\,(\mathit{nRows}\cdot\mathit{nCols})$ and
$\mathrm{consistencyProbability}\,r\,C = (1-r)^{C}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The Information Order}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{extends\_refl}]\label{refl}
$\Extends\,db\,db$ for every $db$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The implication is the identity when $\mathit{big}=\mathit{small}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{extends\_trans}]\label{trans}
$\Extends\,a\,b$ and $\Extends\,b\,c$ imply $\Extends\,a\,c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $c\,p=\some\,v$ obtain $b\,p=\some\,v$, then $a\,p=\some\,v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{extends\_antisymm}]\label{antisymm}
$\Extends\,a\,b$ and $\Extends\,b\,a$ imply $a=b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By extensionality, fix $p$ and case on $a\,p$, $b\,p$; whenever one is $\some\,v$ the
hypotheses force the other to match, and if both are $\none$ they agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{consistentPair\_of\_common\_extension}]\label{sep}
If $\Extends\,g\,a$ and $\Extends\,g\,b$ then $\ConsistentPair\,a\,b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a\,p=\some\,v_1$ and $b\,p=\some\,v_2$, both extension hypotheses give
$g\,p=\some\,v_1$ and $g\,p=\some\,v_2$, so $v_1=v_2$. This direction is choice-free.
\end{proof}

\section{The Binary Gluing is the Join}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{extends\_gluing\_left}]
$\Extends\,(\Glue\,db_1\,db_2)\,db_1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{extends\_gluing\_right}]
If $\ConsistentPair\,db_1\,db_2$ then $\Extends\,(\Glue\,db_1\,db_2)\,db_2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{gluing\_is\_lub}]\label{lub}
If $\Extends\,g\,db_1$ and $\Extends\,g\,db_2$ then
$\Extends\,g\,(\Glue\,db_1\,db_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $p$ with $(\Glue\,db_1\,db_2)\,p=\some\,v$. If $db_1\,p=\some\,v$, use
$\Extends\,g\,db_1$; if $db_1\,p=\none$, the value came from $db_2$, so use
$\Extends\,g\,db_2$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\Glue\,db_1\,db_2$ is the least upper bound (join) of $\{db_1,db_2\}$.

\section{The Arbitrary-Arity Colimit}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{glueFamily\_eq\_none\_iff}]
$\glueFamily\,dbs\,p = \none \iff \forall i,\ dbs\,i\,p = \none$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{glueFamily\_extends}]\label{glueext}
If $\SheafCondition\,dbs$ then $\Extends\,(\glueFamily\,dbs)\,(dbs\,i)$ for all $i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $dbs\,i\,p=\some\,v$, the guard holds and $\glueFamily\,dbs\,p =
dbs\,(\mathrm{choose}\,h)\,p$; the chosen member has a value there, and pairwise
consistency forces it to equal $v$. Uses choice.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{glueFamily\_is\_lub}]\label{gluelub}
If $\Extends\,g\,(dbs\,i)$ for all $i$ then $\Extends\,g\,(\glueFamily\,dbs)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\glueFamily\,dbs\,p=\some\,v$ then $dbs\,j\,p=\some\,v$ for the chosen $j$, and
$\Extends\,g\,(dbs\,j)$ gives $g\,p=\some\,v$. Choice-free.
\end{proof}

By Theorems~\ref{glueext}, \ref{gluelub}, and \ref{antisymm}, $\glueFamily\,dbs$ is
the unique colimit (least common extension) of the diagram.

\section{Main Theorem: Databases Form a Sheaf}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{sheaf\_iff\_common\_extension}]\label{main}
\[
  \SheafCondition\,dbs \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \exists g,\ \forall i,\
  \Extends\,g\,(dbs\,i).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(\Rightarrow)$ Take $g=\glueFamily\,dbs$ and apply Theorem~\ref{glueext}.
$(\Leftarrow)$ For any $i,j$, both members are extended by $g$, so
Theorem~\ref{sep} yields $\ConsistentPair\,(dbs\,i)\,(dbs\,j)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The forward direction is the \emph{gluing axiom} (sufficiency, with explicit
witness $\glueFamily$); the reverse is \emph{separatedness} (necessity). Together
they identify the sheaf condition as the exact criterion for consistent
imputability, and by Theorems~\ref{gluelub} and \ref{antisymm} the merge is the
unique least common extension.
\end{remark}

\section{Progressive Imputation as a Colimit}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{sheaf\_filtration\_auto\_consistent}]
If $\mathit{levels}:\mathrm{Fin}\,d\to\PartialDB$ is monotone in information, then
$\SheafCondition\,\mathit{levels}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $i,j$ assume WLOG $i\le j$; monotonicity sends each filled cell of
$\mathit{levels}\,i$ to the same value in $\mathit{levels}\,j$, so they agree on
overlaps.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{filtration\_colimit\_eq\_top}]
For a sheaf filtration $F$ of positive depth $d$, $\glueFamily\,F.\mathit{level} =
F.\mathit{level}\,\langle d-1\rangle$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The top level extends every level (domains accumulate), so it is a common extension
extended by itself; by Theorem~\ref{gluelub} and antisymmetry the colimit coincides
with it.
\end{proof}

\section{Quantitative Consistency Probability}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{consistency\_prob\_mono\_constraints}]
For $0\le r\le 1$ and $c_1\le c_2$, $(1-r)^{c_2}\le (1-r)^{c_1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{consistency\_prob\_mono\_rate}]
For fixed $c$ and $r_1\le r_2\le 1$, $(1-r_2)^{c}\le (1-r_1)^{c}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{consistency\_prob\_mul}]
$(1-r)^{c_1+c_2} = (1-r)^{c_1}(1-r)^{c_2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
With boundary values $(1-r)^{0}\!\!: \mathrm{consistencyProbability}\,0\,c = 1$ and
$\mathrm{consistencyProbability}\,1\,c = 0$ for $c>0$, and bounds $0\le (1-r)^{c}\le
1$ on $[0,1]$. Since the overlap constraint count grows quadratically in the number
of views and linearly in grid size, $(1-r)^{C}$ decays exponentially as databases
widen.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Pairwise check.} Scan all cells, report a conflict on differing filled
values: $O(\mathit{nRows}\cdot\mathit{nCols})$ per pair.

\paragraph{Family gluing.} For each cell take the first member with a value:
$O(k\cdot\mathit{nRows}\cdot\mathit{nCols})$ for $k$ views.

\paragraph{Sheaf imputation.} Verify the sheaf condition ($O(k^2\cdot
\mathit{nRows}\cdot\mathit{nCols})$); if it holds, return the colimit
$\glueFamily$ as the canonical imputation; if it fails, the failing pair localizes a
genuine contradiction.

\section{Applications and Discussion}

The sheaf condition is a pre-imputation tripwire: failing it certifies that no
consistent fill exists. When it holds, the colimit $\glueFamily$ is the uniquely
defined least common extension---the principled merge. Monotone refinement pipelines
are automatically consistent and converge to their final level. The exponential law
$(1-r)^{C}$ predicts how rare exact global consistency is as a function of width and
noise. Conceptually, \emph{separatedness is free, gluing is the work}: all the
nontrivial content of ``databases form a sheaf'' lives in the forward direction of
Theorem~\ref{main}.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item $\Extends$ as a conditionally complete lattice (bottom $=$ empty database;
    arbitrary meets cellwise; joins on the consistent sublattice via $\glueFamily$).
  \item Vanishing \v{C}ech $H^1$: every 1-cocycle is a coboundary, so the gluing
    obstruction is always trivial.
  \item Nerve connectivity governs gluing complexity, yielding a spanning-forest
    $O(k\cdot\mathit{nRows}\cdot\mathit{nCols})$ algorithm.
  \item Restriction commutes with colimit, making $\glueFamily$ a natural
    transformation.
  \item A sharp consistency-probability threshold from the order/colimit layer.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The slogan ``databases form a sheaf'' is, precisely, the statement that a family of
partial databases admits a consistent merge if and only if it is pairwise
overlap-consistent (Theorem~\ref{main}), and that this merge is the unique colimit in
the information order. Consistent databases have joins; consistent families have
colimits; and progressive imputation is the act of taking those colimits. Missing
data imputation is, in a literal and verified sense, a problem of gluing local
sections into a global one.

\end{document}

